> 
> 

  see the 'na.strings' argument in ?read.table (also applies to read.csv)
  the problem is that having the '.' in there in the first place will
make your numeric variables turn into factors -- you can deal with this via

dat1$y <- as.numeric(as.character(dat1$y))

which will make anything that can't be interpreted as numeric into an NA
(and issue a warning) but it is easier and clearer to specify na.strings
in the first place.

  For what it's worth, you might ask these kinds of questions on the
general R-help list -- they are not specific to mixed models.  (Make
sure to read the Posting Guide first ...)



From Arnaud.Le-Rouzic at legs.cnrs-gif.fr  Fri Feb 18 17:15:04 2011
From: Arnaud.Le-Rouzic at legs.cnrs-gif.fr (Arnaud Le Rouzic)
Date: Fri, 18 Feb 2011 17:15:04 +0100
Subject: [R-sig-ME] Random effects with custom design matrices
Message-ID: <4D5E9B08.8010300@legs.cnrs-gif.fr>

Hi the list,

Sorry if this is a FAQ, I could not find anything corresponding in the 
archive of the list.

Along with some colleagues, we were wondering if there was a simple way 
to customize the design matrix of random effects in lmer. The particular 
context was that of a "diallel" experiment in quantitative genetics, 
which consists in crossing N populations each other to measure the 
hybrid phenotype. The model is

y_ijk =  mu + g_i + g_j + d_ij + e_ijk

and the "funny" thing is that g_i and g_j are 1/2 the effect of the two 
parental populations, i.e. two occurrences of the same random effect. 
d_ij is the interaction effect, and it is non-0 only if i and j are 
different.

It is quite straightforward to write the design matrices. Let's consider 
a simple data set, like:

   father mother        phen
1      1      1  1.56
2      1      1 -0.09
3      1      2  2.18
4      1      2  0.73
5      1      3  0.87
6      1      3 -0.06

If the model is written Y = X1 G + X2 D, matrix X1 is

1    1    0    0
2    1    0    0
3    0.5    0.5    0
4    0.5    0.5    0
5    0.5    0    0.5
6    0.5    0    0.5
etc.,

and matrix X2 is

1    0    0    0
2    0    0    0
